    "text": "An embodied agent that uses a differential entropy measure of predictability of its own future actions given its memory as a criterion for memory pruning outperforms FIFO and random eviction in zero-shot transfer to procedurally varied environments, as measured by task success rate.",
    "topic": "Predictability-based memory pruning for robust planning under shift"
  },
  {
    "text": "Training a memory injection policy with a min-max objective where the memory agent seeks to maximize the adversary's next-action prediction error, while the adversarial predictor is trained on the agent's trajectory, leads to plan coherence (measured by steps-to-goal) under environment perturbations that is at least 20% higher than a baseline that always injects the most recent observation.",
    "topic": "Adversarial unpredictability for memory injection strategy"
  }
]
